We report the cells of Table~\ref{tab:design} for the unchanged \texttt{shap} Kernel explainer with \texttt{l1\_reg=False} unless another arm is named; the other arms are in the appendix.
The \emph{reference budget} of a cell is the \texttt{shap} default $m=2d+2048$ where that samples ($d\ge16$) and, for $d\le10$, where the default enumerates all coalitions and is exact, the largest sampled budget (half of all coalitions).
Intervals resample instances; the five datasets are five observations of the setting, so we report each and pool nothing.

\subsection{The reference is exact and the randomness is where we expect it}
\label{sec:validity}
Exact enumeration and interventional TreeSHAP on the same background agree to a relative $L_2$ distance of at most $4\times10^{-6}$ in every forest and boosted-tree cell except Pima RF (median $8\times10^{-4}$, maximum $0.04$; we did not resolve its cause; the enumeration remains the reference).
For $d\le10$ the \texttt{shap} default equals the reference to $5\times10^{-15}$.
For logistic regression the \texttt{shap} Kernel and permutation runs have no variance (run-to-run standard deviation below $10^{-14}$ of $\|\phi\|$), and the paired Kernel estimator has the variance expected of a sampled estimator, so the control behaves as it should.
The paired Kernel and antithetic permutation estimators and \texttt{l1\_reg=False} are unbiased by the statistic of Section~\ref{sec:design} (median $T\approx1$), while \texttt{num\_features(10)} is not (median $T=440$), and \texttt{auto} is for some instances (maximum $T>2000$); we report the biased arms separately.

\subsection{At the default budget one run gets the top-3 set, except near ties}
\label{sec:headline}
Figure~\ref{fig:headline} is the headline.
At the \texttt{shap} default budget of the two $d\ge16$ datasets, the share of instances with $P_c^{\varepsilon}\ge0.9$ is $1.00$ in all eight cells, and the share with strict $P_c\ge0.9$ is $1.00$ for logistic regression and $0.90$--$0.97$ for the other models.
At the reference budget of the $d\le10$ datasets the strict share is $0.93$--$1.00$ on ACS Income, $0.87$--$1.00$ on Pima and $0.79$--$1.00$ on GMSC, the lowest being the random forest on GMSC ($0.79$); the tolerance-aware share is $1.00$ in all $20$ cells.
The tolerance does the work: with $\varepsilon=0$ the share falls by up to $0.21$, and with $\varepsilon\ge0.02$ it is $1.00$ everywhere (Table~\ref{tab:sens}); the headline does not depend on the threshold ($0.8$, $0.95$) or on $k\in\{1,3,5\}$ at $\varepsilon=0.05$ (Table~\ref{tab:sens}).
The default is not the same as the other \texttt{shap} settings: with \texttt{num\_features(10)}, which selects ten features by a lasso path and is biased, the strict share at the default budget falls to $0.68$--$0.93$ on the six non-linear $d\ge16$ cells and to $0.78$ and $0.87$ on logistic regression (Table~\ref{tab:sens}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figures/fig2_headline.pdf}
\caption{Single-run reliability of the top-3 set against the budget $m$ (\texttt{shap} Kernel, \texttt{l1\_reg=False}; $R=30$--$100$ runs per instance, $40$--$100$ instances). Top: share of instances with $P_c^{\varepsilon}\ge0.9$ at $\varepsilon=0.05$ (filled, band: $95\%$ instance-bootstrap interval) and with strict $P_c\ge0.9$ (open, dotted); dashed vertical line: \texttt{shap} default $2d+2048$. Middle: mean Pearson correlation between a run and the exact $\phi$. Bottom: share of instances with $P_c\le x$ at the reference budget printed under each panel. The tolerance-aware share is one wherever the correlation is above $0.98$, and the gap to the strict share is a thin tail of instances with $P_c<1$.}
\label{fig:headline}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Failures are near ties; aggregate agreement is high except at small budgets}
\label{sec:margin}
